# Database migrations

ServerWatch uses Flask-Migrate and Alembic to manage a single SQLAlchemy database.
Committed revisions are the source of truth for schema changes. Production uses
MySQL 8; automated migration tests also exercise SQLite.

## Revision history

| Revision | Parent | Scope |
|---|---|---|
| c99481d201d8 | None | Initial ServerWatch schema |
| 4e2c9d8f610a | c99481d201d8 | Guided server setup, SSH key metadata, host-key pinning, and installation lifecycle fields |

The setup-wizard migration preserves existing SSH credentials, Telegram group
bindings, and monitoring history. Existing clients do not need to be reinstalled.

## Apply committed revisions

On the deployed central VPS, back up the database and original encryption key,
stop application services, and run:

    sudo /opt/serverwatch/.venv/bin/python /opt/serverwatch/scripts/manage.py upgrade
    sudo -u serverwatch /opt/serverwatch/.venv/bin/python /opt/serverwatch/scripts/manage.py status

The upgrade helper reads /etc/serverwatch/serverwatch.env and the root-only
/etc/serverwatch/migration.env. It then drops operating-system privileges to
serverwatch before running Flask CLI with the migration database account.
Runtime services use a separate account without schema-change privileges.

For the complete stop, backup, upgrade, and restart sequence, see sections 7 and
16 of the root README.md. Do not regenerate the environment or ENCRYPTION_KEY
when upgrading an existing installation.

## Create a revision during development

Use a separate development database and an environment with DDL privileges:

    python -m flask --app run db current
    python -m flask --app run db migrate -m "Describe the schema change"
    python -m flask --app run db upgrade
    python -m flask --app run db heads

Review generated upgrade() and downgrade() operations, especially data changes,
constraints, indexes, and MySQL-specific behavior. Autogeneration is a starting
point and may require manual edits. Commit the reviewed revision together with
its model changes and relevant documentation.

Run the migration tests against a disposable database:

    python -m pytest tests/test_migrations.py -q

SQLite tests do not establish production MySQL compatibility. Validate material
schema changes against the target MySQL version before deployment.

## Operational constraints

- Do not run db init or db migrate on production; apply the committed revisions.
- Do not use db.create_all() or stamp head to bypass a failed migration.
- MySQL DDL may leave partial changes after an error. Inspect both the actual
  schema and alembic_version before planning recovery.
- Do not run migrations concurrently with the backup job or application writes.
- A downgrade may remove data. Review its implementation and verify a restorable
  backup before using it; a downgrade is not a substitute for disaster recovery.

## Directory layout

    env.py          Flask application/database integration and Alembic context
    alembic.ini     Migration logging and Alembic configuration
    script.py.mako  Template used when creating new revisions
    versions/       Ordered, committed schema revisions
